tle emotional development.\",\"group_id\":0},{\"id\":\"s000021\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":7,\"style\":5,\"grammar\":9,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"Mateo contributes a written rhythm despite not wanting to perform, giving Nia a workable piece; the paper-between-drums ending is unexplained.\",\"group_id\":1},{\"id\":\"s000034\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":7,\"style\":5,\"grammar\":9,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"Mateo contributes a written rhythm despite not wanting to perform, giving Nia a workable piece; the paper-between-drums ending is unexplained.\",\"group_id\":1},{\"id\":\"s000048\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":8,\"style\":5,\"grammar\":9,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"The solo choice resolves Mateo’s reluctance, and the performance ends clearly, though the brief, repetitive dialogue leaves little emotional development.\",\"group_id\":0}],\"diversity\":{\"premise\":2,\"causal_path\":3,\"ending\":3,\"language\":2,\"overall\":2},\"diversity_evidence\":\"All ten share the premise of Nia wanting a drum duet while Mateo is uneasy about an audience. Most (s000014, s000028, s000023, s000036, s000053, s000048) have Nia perform alone; s000058, s000021, and s000034 vary this by having Mateo write a rhythm for her, while s000017 moves the duet to a private setting. The repeated wording and closely related resolutions make the set highly similar; these sampled stories do not establish diversity across the full generator.\",\"plot_groups\":[{\"group_id\":0,\"summary\":\"Mateo declines an audience duet, so Nia practices and performs a solo, which Mateo applauds.\"},{\"group_id\":1,\"summary\":\"Mateo avoids performing by writing a rhythm for Nia, who practices and plays it for Dalia.\"},{\"group_id\":2,\"summary\":\"Mateo is uncomfortable performing publicly, so he and Nia play their duet privately.\"}]}",
